# `nearest`: use alone says that `p` is a list of numbers, so the loop over the centers is built once for a list and once for every view. In a copy of a loop that is told apart no loop was ("one level at
# a time, else k^depth"), and `r`, which the turn around finds, was read by key in every turn of the innermost loop: 24,240 > 60,261 instructions a call. The innermost loop holds no loop to multiply: it
# tells apart what the turn around it has found; what stood in front of the loop around had its chance there.
# f1 = between, f2 = nearest, f3 = nearestOfTwo
# RUN(COUNT): compile InnermostLoopsToldApartInCopiesOfLoopsAround
# COUNT-NOT: statistic InnermostLoopsToldApartInCopiesOfLoopsAround 0
# RUN(SHELL): run-in-shell globalThis.sink = print;
